Let 'return' and 'yield' take a bind(...) expression

The language manual gives

    return_statement => return [ expression ]
    yield_statement  => yield [ expression ]

and lists closure creation, bind(...), among the expressions. But
'return bind(f(_))' fails to parse:

    'end' expected, but found 'bind' (in routine/iter body)

return_stmt and yield_stmt in Compiler/Front/parse.sa only parse a
value when is_expr_start(next) holds, and is_expr_start lists every
token the primary expression parser accepts except bind_tok. bind was
added in 1.1 to replace #ROUT(...) and #ITER(...), which start with
sharp_tok and so were already covered; the list was not updated. Seeing
no value, 'return' expects the statement to end and stops at 'bind'.

Elsewhere bind(...) goes through the full expression parser, which is
why it already works after ':=' or as an argument, and why writing
'return (bind(f(_)))' works: lparen_tok is on the list.

Adding bind_tok makes is_stmt_start accept it too, since that falls
back to is_expr_start; a statement that is just a bind(...) is then
rejected by the type checker, as any other value used as a statement
is, instead of by the parser.

--- a/Compiler/Front/parse.sa	2026-11-21 12:25:54.526583313 +1100
+++ b/Compiler/Front/parse.sa	2026-11-21 12:25:54.538966682 +1100
@@ -1197,9 +1197,12 @@
    end;
 
    is_expr_start (t:TOKEN):BOOL is
+      -- Every token the primary expression parser accepts must be listed
+      -- here, or 'return' and 'yield' will not see that a value follows.
       case t.val
       when self_tok, ident_tok, bang_tok, iter_bang_tok, SAME_tok, void_tok,
-         minus_tok, not_tok, new_tok, sharp_tok, vbar_tok, exception_tok, 
+         minus_tok, not_tok, new_tok, sharp_tok, bind_tok, vbar_tok,
+         exception_tok, 
          initial_tok, result_tok, while_tok, until_tok, break_tok, 
          true_tok, false_tok, lchar_tok, lstr_tok, lint_tok, lflt_tok, 
          lparen_tok, lbracket_tok,
